#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422 :topic/difficulty 1.18463012388646 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428274 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 151->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "f3a09ebe-2d8e-5a69-8681-eb398a98c3dd" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059584 :question/id #uuid "0a9e6824-677a-5fae-9867-1701e7d8378f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4697" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\n  \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=0 \\\\ y &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a10cafc6-9da7-5ffc-a0c7-686a7638fbd9" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 2x2 Augmented Matrix to REF Using Gaussian Elimination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059585 :question/id #uuid "1eea81be-26d7-57dd-a622-d66300de7f4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4698" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+5y &=5 \\\\ x-6y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-5y &=-5 \\\\ y &=-10,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07abc1cc-5794-51a6-bad4-40565eae52be" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] :topic/math-academy-id 151 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049409}] :topic/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/id #uuid "38bc1722-543b-58cb-b448-fb93b0e623de" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073480 :question/id #uuid "f2105fec-c85b-5928-92e6-95ed80f1326c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14009" :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + 2y &=4 \\\\ 2x + y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=4 \\\\ 2x+y &=4 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073481 :question/id #uuid "b1d34db3-7f30-5a8c-b233-fd955ac656f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14011" :question/problem "At how many points do the following lines intersect?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n10y - 5x &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=2 \\\\ 10y - 5x &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fab4792-0fe1-5a5c-8754-509af469da68" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073482 :question/id #uuid "ab09c3eb-6813-57c6-891b-bf20196cbe93" :question/math-academy-id "e-14014" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x+2y &= 2 \\\\[6pt]\n6x+4y &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + 2y &=2 \\\\ 6x + 4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x+4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points"}] :topic/math-academy-id 493 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050673}] :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}
